/* Generated by scripts/build-manual-index.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
window.MANUAL_INDEX = [{"url":"appendices.html#glossary","title":"Glossary","section":"appendices","text":"Node One card on the canvas. Each does one thing: send a message, build a machine, pay the player. Wire The line joining two nodes. Wires decide what runs and in what order; where the nodes sit decides nothing. Socket The point a wire attaches to. Every socket takes one kind of wire and refuses the others. Palette The list of nodes down the left. Drag one out, or click it to drop one in the middle. Inspector The panel on the right, with its Node , Quest and Mod tabs. Field One setting on a node. Every field is a dropdown, a switch, a box to type in or a colour. Hint The grey sentence under a field. It is the editor's own wording, and this handbook quotes it rather than paraphrasing. Message "},{"url":"appendices.html#shortcuts","title":"Keyboard and mouse","section":"appendices","text":"Keys work anywhere. Gestures are on the canvas. The editor shows this same list under Shortcuts . Editing Undo Ctrl Z Redo Ctrl Shift Z or Ctrl Y Copy / cut / paste the selected nodes Ctrl C · X · V Duplicate the selected nodes Ctrl D Delete the selected nodes or wires Delete Save the draft now Ctrl S Adding nodes Place a node where you drop it drag from the palette Add a node at the viewport centre click a palette card Search for a node at the pointer Shift A Search for a node here right-click the canvas Create a node the wire fits drag a wire onto empty canvas Selecting Select one node click it Add to or toggle the selection Shift or Ctrl + click Box-select; any overlap counts drag on empt"},{"url":"appendices.html#limits","title":"What this editor cannot do yet","section":"appendices","text":"Stated plainly, because each of these has cost somebody an afternoon. None is a bug; each is a boundary, and the editor does not always tell you when you cross one. It cannot give an objective two triggers Only the first event wired to a trigger is used. A second one is ignored without a message. If you need two ways to finish, use two objectives. It will not finish a quest without a finish path Add Complete quest to a success ending, or switch on Complete automatically . On quest complete runs after that path. It cannot fix Bettercap's access-point name display Create Wi-Fi now ships as a real access point. In the current game build, Bettercap may show no network name after you target a mod"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-list","title":"The 99 events","section":"appendices","text":"Every event a When event node can wait for, grouped the way the editor groups them. Type part of a name into the node's Game event picker rather than reading this list — it is here so you can see the shape of what is available."},{"url":"appendices.html#events-recon","title":"Reconnaissance & terminal (18)","section":"appendices","text":"Terminal.Cat Terminal.Cd Terminal.Command Terminal.Dig Terminal.DnsHistory Terminal.Explorer Terminal.Geoip Terminal.Ifconfig Terminal.InstallPackage Terminal.Ls Terminal.Lynx.Lookup Terminal.Lynx.Search Terminal.Mxlookup Terminal.NmapScan Terminal.Nslookup Terminal.Openssl Terminal.Ping Terminal.Whois"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-web","title":"Directory brute-force, browser & HTTP (7)","section":"appendices","text":"Browser.Meta Browser.WebsiteOpened Http.CollaboratorHit Http.Intercepted Http.Request Http.Response Terminal.Dirhunter"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-access","title":"Access & exploitation (19)","section":"appendices","text":"Metasploit.Event Metasploit.Event.Try Metasploit.Meterpreter.Connected Metasploit.Msfconsole Metasploit.Rootgrab Metasploit.Search Metasploit.SetOption Metasploit.ShowOptions Metasploit.Use Meterpreter.Download RemoteConnection.Disconnected RemoteConnection.Established Terminal.FTP.Connect Terminal.Hydra Terminal.Hydra.Try Terminal.SSH.Connected Terminal.SSH.Disconnected Terminal.SSH.FileDownload Terminal.SSH.Shutdown"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-cracking","title":"Cracking & vuln scanning (9)","section":"appendices","text":"Fern.FindPassword Hashcat John.DecryptHash Nuclei.Item Nuclei.Results Sqlmap.DumpTable Sqlmap.ListTables Subfinder.Results Subfinder.Try"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-wifi","title":"Bettercap & Wi-Fi (8)","section":"appendices","text":"Bettercap.Close Bettercap.NetProbe Bettercap.NetShow Bettercap.Open Bettercap.WifiDeAuth Bettercap.WifiRecon Network.WifiConnected Network.WifiDisconnected"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-network","title":"Network & infrastructure (7)","section":"appendices","text":"Database.Connected Database.DataUpdate Network.PortChanges Network.UserActivity NetworkPacketTransfer PFSense.Changes PFSense.Login"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-files","title":"Files (4)","section":"appendices","text":"Files.Deleted Files.Open Files.Transfer Python3.ExecFile"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-mail","title":"E-mail (6)","section":"appendices","text":"Mail.AccountCreated Mail.AccountLoggedIn Mail.MailboxOpened Mail.Read Mail.Received Mail.Sent"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-social","title":"Social & chat (11)","section":"appendices","text":"Kisscord.FriendAdded Kisscord.Messaging Twotter.AccountCreated Twotter.AccountLogin Twotter.AccountLogout Twotter.Post Twotter.PostSeen Twotter.ProfileSeen WeeChat.Connected WeeChat.Disconnected WeeChat.Message"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-world","title":"Bank, quest & misc (10)","section":"appendices","text":"AppStore.Downloaded Bank.AccountCreated Bank.Logout Bank.Transfer BCC.News.Opened Game.SessionStarted Process.Killed Quest.Claimed Wireshark.Started Wireshark.Stopped Names are dotted and capitalised. They come from the game, not from this editor, so they read like Terminal.Lynx.Search rather than like a sentence. You never type one by hand."},{"url":"appendices.html#version","title":"Which handbook version is this","section":"appendices","text":"This handbook documents editor build 2026-09-18.r188 . The same stamp appears in the footer of every page, and the editor writes it into every mod it exports. To check your editor Open the canvas Debug panel. The build stamp is the first thing in it. To check a mod you exported Open dist/mod.js inside the .zip . The stamp is in the header comment. See the build stamp . Why it matters A handbook for an older build can describe a field that has since moved. If your stamp is newer than this one, the pages are probably still right, but the export window is the authority on what ships. ← When something goes wrong HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-09-18.r188 · every figur"},{"url":"checking.html#where-messages-appear","title":"Where messages appear","section":"checking","text":"On the node A small badge on the card, visible from across the canvas. This is how you find a problem in a quest too big to read node by node. In the inspector Under the field the message is about, when you have that node selected. Field messages only show while the node is selected, so they cannot nag at you from the other side of the canvas. On the canvas, as a chip A counter beside the canvas tools. It reads No issues when the quest is clean, otherwise 1 blocking, 3 to review — blocking first, and only the counts that are not zero. Hover it for every message in full, each with its next step. Red means at least one blocking problem; amber means none. Inside a panel Ten further messages liv"},{"url":"checking.html#levels","title":"What the two colours mean","section":"checking","text":"Amber Unfinished, or fragile. Exporting still works, and the player may never notice. Fix it before you ship, but nothing is broken right now. Red The player will hit this. Either the quest cannot be finished, or something you built will not survive the trip into the game. Fix it before you export. There is no third level. A quest with no messages has nothing the editor can find fault with, which is not the same as a quest that plays well — only playing it tells you that. See Dry run for the closest thing to a test drive."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-index","title":"Messages on the canvas and in the inspector","section":"checking","text":"Each entry below quotes the editor's own wording. The exact text is what the search box matches, so copy it from the status bar rather than paraphrasing it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-unreachable","title":"Unreachable","section":"checking","text":"Nothing leads to this node. Wire it to the chain that should run it — nodes do nothing until something points at them. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the status bar. What it means The node is on the canvas and is never run. A node only does something when another node points at it, or when it is one of the lifecycle nodes that starts a chain by itself. What to do Drag a wire from the node that should run it into this node's input. That is usually the last node of your Quest start chain. If the node is left over from an idea you dropped, delete it instead."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-no-trigger","title":"No trigger","section":"checking","text":"Nothing completes this objective. Wire a “When event” node into its trigger socket, or the player can never finish the quest. Colour Red Where On the objective, and in the status bar. What it means The player receives the objective and can never tick it over. There is no condition attached to it, so nothing in the game can complete it. A quest with an objective like this cannot be finished. What to do Add a When event node, pick the game event that means the player did the thing, and wire its When socket into the objective's Trigger socket."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-dead-end","title":"Dead end","section":"checking","text":"The “…” outcome goes nowhere, so the quest stalls if the player takes it. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the status bar. What it means The node offers more than one way out and at least one of them is unwired. The wording names the outcome, so Yes or No tells you which side is missing. The editor writes a different version for two node types, because the same shape means something different in each. On a Sequence It reads The \"…\" output goes nowhere, so that step of the sequence does nothing. A step with no wire is a beat that never lands. On Manual input It reads The \"…\" outcome goes nowhere, so a wrong answer shows the failure message and the player tries again. That is the usual d"},{"url":"checking.html#msg-nothing-scheduled","title":"Nothing scheduled","section":"checking","text":"No time (or no full date) is set, so the timer fires the moment the story reaches it — nothing waits. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the status bar. What it means The Timer has no time set, so it does not actually wait: the story carries straight on through it. That is probably not the timer you meant to build. What to do Set a time in the node's Days , Hours or Minutes fields — or a full date in Year , Month and Day — or remove the node if the story should carry on."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-timer-nothing-to-do","title":"The timer has nothing to do","section":"checking","text":"When the time comes, the timer has nowhere to go — the story stops there. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the status bar. What it means The Timer has a time, but nothing is wired to its Out socket. When the time comes, the timer fires and the story has no next step. What to do Wire the Out socket to the node that should run when the time comes, or leave it unwired if the story is meant to end there."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-only-runs-on-completion","title":"Only runs on completion","section":"checking","text":"This is wired, but it only runs after the quest is marked complete. This graph has no Complete quest node yet. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means You have built follow-up work on On quest complete , but the graph has no visible finish node. That path waits until something marks the quest complete. What to do Add Complete quest to the final story beat, or use Complete automatically or the player's Complete button from Behaviour."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-empty","title":"Empty","section":"checking","text":"Nothing is wired to “…” That is fine if you do not need it — delete the node to clear this. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the status bar. What it means One of the four lifecycle nodes is on the canvas with nothing after it, so it runs and changes nothing. The message names the node in the quotes. What to do Wire the nodes that should run at that moment to its output. On Quest start that is usually your briefing message first. If you do not need the node, delete it and the message goes with it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-no-machine","title":"No machine set","section":"checking","text":"No machine set — this node does nothing until it points at one. Colour Amber Where In the inspector, under Device IP or Protected IP . What it means A Change port or Firewall rule node has no machine to act on. The field is blank, which usually means you have not got to it yet rather than that something is broken. What to do Type the target address token to aim it at the machine your network created, or pick Random — the quest's network on a firewall rule."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-no-network","title":"No network creates that machine","section":"checking","text":"This is set to a machine, but no network in this quest creates one for it to act on. Colour Amber Where In the inspector, under the address field. What it means The address is filled in, and nothing in the quest brings a machine with that address into the world. The node points at a machine that will not exist. What to do Add a Create network node from World building and point this at a device in it. If you do not need the node, remove it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-network","title":"Files aimed at a network that does not exist","section":"checking","text":"This “Place files” node puts files on a remote device, but no network in this quest exists for that device to live on — export would drop the files. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The files have nowhere to land. Exporting drops them rather than failing, so the player gets a quest with the clue missing and no error anywhere. What to do Add a Create network node and give it the device this node is aimed at."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-device","title":"Files aimed at an address nothing uses","section":"checking","text":"No device in this quest uses that address, so these files would be dropped when you export. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The address is typed in and matches no device your network creates. With no address at all, the editor writes No address set, so the files have nowhere to land instead. Either way, export drops the files. What to do The message lists the addresses your quest does create. Point this node at one of them, or at the same token you gave the network."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-user","title":"Files aimed at a device with no account","section":"checking","text":"That device has no user account, and files mount under a user's home folder — export would drop the files. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The device exists, but there is nobody logged into it, and files land in a user's home folder. Export drops them. What to do Add a user account to that device in your Create network node."},{"url":"checking.html#panel-messages","title":"Messages inside a panel","section":"checking","text":"These ten do not come from the canvas analysis. Each one is written by the editor you are working in, about the thing in front of you. They never appear on a node badge and never reach the export report, which makes them the kind of problem that goes unnoticed. They are listed here in full."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-objective-trigger","title":"An objective the player can never complete","section":"checking","text":"1 objective has no trigger wired in, so the player can never complete it. Where The Quest tab, in Health at the bottom. What it means Nothing can tick that objective off. Not the story flow, because nothing reaches it, and no event, because none is wired to its trigger. The quest cannot be finished. What to do Wire a When event into its Trigger socket, or run a flow wire into its In socket. Objectives explains the difference."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-router-no-way-in","title":"A router the player cannot reach","section":"checking","text":"This router has no way in: set a model for `fern`, or enable support-mail recovery. Where In the device tree, under the router. What it means Every route into that router is closed. The player can see it exists and can do nothing with it, which usually stalls the quest without any message telling them why. What to do Give fern a model, or switch on support-mail recovery. Either one opens a door."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-ssh-guest","title":"An exploit that lands in the wrong account","section":"checking","text":"The SSH exploit lands in a guest account when it finds one, so the player may need to switch user or crack a password to reach the accounts you wrote. Turn this off for a machine that should have only the accounts listed here. Where In the device tree, under a machine with the exploit enabled. What it means This one is a reminder rather than a fault. The player gets in, but not necessarily as the account you wrote your files into. A quest that reads as broken is often this. What to do Leave it on if switching user is part of the puzzle. Turn it off if the machine should hold only the accounts you listed."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-table-no-name","title":"A database table the game will skip","section":"checking","text":"This table has no name, so the game will skip it. Where Under a table in a database editor. What it means The rows you typed will not exist in the game. The player will search a table that was never created. What to do Name the table. The rows survive as soon as it has one."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-event-detail","title":"A condition that tests a detail the event does not have","section":"checking","text":"Not one of this event's known details Where A small amber warning icon beside a condition's value field. Hover it for this text. What it means You are comparing against a detail name this event does not carry. The comparison will never be true, so the branch never fires. What to do Pick a detail from the list the editor offers for that event. If the one you want is absent, this event cannot answer the question you are asking."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-addon-not-set-up","title":"An addon card that runs nothing","section":"checking","text":"Not set up yet — this card doesn't know which tool action it runs. Delete it and drag the action in again from the palette's “Editor Mods” group. As it stands it does nothing. Where On an addon card in the inspector. What it means The card was made without the addon it belongs to loaded. It has a shape but no action behind it. What to do Delete the card and drag the action in again, with the addon loaded. Editing the existing card will not fix it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-addon-needs-mod","title":"An addon the player does not have","section":"checking","text":"Players need the Example Pack game mod installed for this to work — say so in your quest description. Where On an addon card, in both the tool-response and pack-data editors. What it means The node works for you because you have the addon. A player without it gets a quest that quietly does nothing, and no message tells them what is missing. What to do Name the addon in your quest description. Nothing in the editor can say it for you."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-pack-not-loaded","title":"A pack you cannot re-pick from","section":"checking","text":"The Example Pack pack isn't loaded on this machine. Everything you chose here is saved in the project, so the node keeps working — load the pack again to pick a different data shape. Where In the pack-data editor. What it means Your choice is safe and the node still works. You cannot change the data shape until the pack is loaded again. What to do Nothing, unless you want a different shape. Load the addon to change it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-timed-conversation","title":"A conversation that plays live","section":"checking","text":"Timed conversations are sent through the game's live messaging API, one message at a time with the delays you set. Player replies, uploads and “unlocks after” steps only work in the normal declarative script, and the game does not clean live messages up with the quest. Worth an in-game check before you ship it. Where Under Play when the story reaches this node on a dialogue node, once it is switched on. What it means Live playback costs you three things: replies stop working, uploads stop working, and the messages stay on the player's phone after the quest ends. What to do Leave it off unless you need the conversation to land on a specific beat. If you need it, test the quest in the game."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-page-unlisted","title":"A page nobody can find","section":"checking","text":"Not in search results — only a direct URL (or dirhunter) leads here. Where In the website builder, above the preview of an unlisted page. What it means Deliberate, and often what you want: the player has to be told the address or find it by brute force. It is a problem only if you expected the page to be searchable. What to do Nothing, if hiding the page is the point. Otherwise list it, and hand the player a way to reach it."},{"url":"checking.html#pre-export","title":"The report before you export","section":"checking","text":"Exporting runs a second set of checks that need the whole mod in front of them: what your websites do to each other, whether a machine can actually be broken into. The report lists everything it found before the .zip is written, so you can go back and fix it. Red entries mean the export dropped or changed something. Amber entries mean it shipped what you built, and what you built is probably not what you meant."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-network-shape","title":"A network the game cannot build","section":"checking","text":"… has machines behind it, but only a router or a splitter can hold other machines. Colour Red What it means You have hung a device behind something that cannot host one. The game has no way to build the shape. What to do Put a router or a splitter between them in your Create network node. Two more red entries come from the same place. One names a machine carrying firewall rules when only a firewall device enforces them, so the rules are ignored. The other names a machine with a login service open and no user accounts, so the player cannot break in — there is nothing to log in as."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-tool-version","title":"A version a tool will not read","section":"checking","text":"… metasploit needs three numbers (for example “OpenSSH 7.2.0”) — it rejects anything else. Colour Red What it means A vulnerability's version is written in a form the game's attack tool refuses to read, so the player cannot exploit it. The weakness is on the list and does nothing. What to do Write the version as three numbers, the way the tool expects."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-domain-clash","title":"Two websites on one domain","section":"checking","text":"… is the host of … websites in this mod. Domains are global — two sites on one host will fight over it. Colour Red What it means Domains belong to the whole game, not to your quest. Two sites claiming one host means whichever loads last wins, and the other disappears. What to do Give one of them its own hostname. Two related sites read better as subdomains of one host anyway. Three more entries come from the same checks. A red one names a host with two pages at the same path, which ship as two definitions of one address. Another names a page whose path does not start at the host root. An amber one warns that a placeholder domain ships as a real site any player can find, and another mod may a"},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-conversation","title":"A conversation nobody reaches","section":"checking","text":"… a conversation is set to “play when the story reaches this node” but nothing is wired into it — it never plays. Colour Amber What it means A Dialogue node waits for the story to arrive and the story never does. The player never has the conversation. What to do Wire the node into the chain, or set the conversation to start some other way."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-addon","title":"An addon node that is not set up","section":"checking","text":"… a “Give data to an addon” node is not set up yet. Colour Amber What it means A node that depends on a community addon has no pack or node chosen, so it exports as something that does nothing. The same check covers Addon node . What to do Open the node and pick the pack and the shape or node it should use. If the addon is not installed, install it first."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-kisscord","title":"An upload that becomes a message","section":"checking","text":"… Kisscord uploads compile to a “[uploaded file …]” message. Colour Amber What it means A file attached to a Kisscord conversation does not reach the player as a file. It arrives as a line of text standing in for it. Nothing is lost; the player does not get the attachment itself. What to do Nothing, if a line of text is enough. If the player needs the actual file, put it somewhere with Place files and have the conversation point at it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-account","title":"A Twotter node with nobody to post as","section":"checking","text":"… the Twotter node has no account yet. Colour Red What it means A Twotter node posts from an account, and this one has none chosen — or the account it names has since been deleted from the mod. Exported as it stands, the node posts nothing at all, and a clue the player was meant to find is not there. What to do Pick an account in the node's Account field. If the character does not exist yet, open Manage accounts in the Twotter window and add it — accounts belong to the whole mod, so one character can post across several quests."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-empty","title":"A Twotter node that posts nothing","section":"checking","text":"… the Twotter node posts nothing — it has no tweet rows. Colour Amber What it means The node is wired into the story and reaches the player, but there is nothing for it to post. The same check covers a tweet row with neither text nor a picture — that one is a profile with a blank post on it. What to do Write the post, or delete the node if the story has moved on without it. ← How do I… Exporting and installing → ↑ back to top HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-09-18.r188 · every message on this page is quoted from the editor's own source."},{"url":"concepts.html#quest-map","title":"Your quest map","section":"concepts","text":"A project holds three things: quests, websites, and the mod settings that give the whole thing a name. A quest is a map of nodes joined by wires, laid out on a canvas you can pan and zoom. You build that map the way you would sketch one on paper. Drag a node in from the palette. Type into its fields in the inspector. Draw a wire from one of its sockets to a socket on another node. Where the nodes sit changes nothing. Two nodes side by side are not neighbours to the game; only a wire makes them neighbours. The order your story runs in comes from wires, and from nothing else. The canvas invites you to forget this, because a tidy layout looks like a sequence. Some things on the canvas are drawi"},{"url":"concepts.html#lifecycle","title":"When your quest runs","section":"concepts","text":"Four nodes cover the entire life of a quest. They are the only ones the game starts by itself, and nothing runs until one of them does. Quest start Runs once, when the quest begins. Setup goes here: build the network, place the files, tell the player what has happened. On start & reload Runs when the quest is claimed, and again every time the game loads. Anything that must be listening goes here, because a listener set up in Quest start is gone after a reload. On quest complete Runs after the quest is marked complete. A Complete quest node, auto-complete, or the player's Complete button can do that. On quest abandoned Runs if the player gives up. Cleanup goes here. You can use more than one "},{"url":"concepts.html#wire-kinds","title":"Wires have kinds","section":"concepts","text":"There are four kinds of wire. Each one answers a different question, which is why they look different. Wire Colour The question Then Blue What happens next in the story? When Cyan What has to happen in the game? Unlocks Amber, dashed Which objective comes after this one? Data Violet, dotted Where does this value come from? A socket accepts only its own kind of wire. Dragging a flow wire at a condition socket will not connect. That is deliberate rather than fussy: a wire meaning \"and then\" arriving at something that asked \"when\" would be a question the game has no answer for."},{"url":"concepts.html#objectives","title":"Objectives are how the player knows what to do","section":"concepts","text":"An objective is a line in the player's quest panel. It is the only part of your quest the player is told about, so it is where the story stops being yours and starts being theirs. An objective completes in one of two ways. Either the story flow reaches it, which ticks it off at that moment. Or an event is wired into its Trigger socket, and the objective completes when the player does that thing in the game. The second is the one you want most of the time. It is the difference between \"the story says you did it\" and \"you did it\". Wiring a When event node to an objective's trigger is how a quest notices real play instead of assuming it. Two details the game is strict about. Only the first even"},{"url":"concepts.html#events","title":"Events are how your quest hears the player","section":"concepts","text":"An event fires when the player does something in the game. Searching for something, logging into a bank, cracking a password, downloading a file — each of those has a name, and there are 99 of them in 10 groups. A When event node picks one of those names. When it fires, your quest hears about it and carries on down the wire. That single node is how a quest becomes reactive rather than scripted. Some events carry information with them. Terminal.Lynx.Search tells you what the player typed, which is how a quest reacts to a specific search rather than to searching in general. The groups are named after the part of the game they come from: terminal, browser, cracking, files, e-mail, social, and t"},{"url":"concepts.html#what-export-means","title":"What exporting means","section":"concepts","text":"Your project is the editable thing: the canvas, the fields, the websites. The game cannot play a project. Exporting turns one into a mod, which is a folder the game loads. These two are not interchangeable, and treating them as the same thing is how work gets lost. The mod is finished output. You cannot open it again and carry on building. Everything about the file it produces, where it goes and what to do about updates is on the exporting and installing page."},{"url":"concepts.html#permissions","title":"Permissions, in plain words","section":"concepts","text":"A permission is a promise about what your mod is allowed to touch. The game shows the list to the player when they install, so it is a promise the player reads. You never pick permissions. The editor works out which ones your nodes need, from seven in total, and asks for exactly those. Delete the node and the permission disappears with it. Permission In plain words network The mod puts machines, names and files into the game's world. filesystem The mod writes files onto the player's own machine. shell The mod runs commands, answers for the game's tools, or adds a command of its own. events The mod watches for what the player does, or starts another quest. mail The mod sends messages to the p"},{"url":"concepts.html#why-no-complete","title":"How quests finish","section":"concepts","text":"A quest finishes when something marks it complete. The clearest way is the Complete quest node, placed at the end of a success path. You can also turn on autoComplete . Then the game closes the quest when every objective is done. Use that when the objectives alone prove success. On quest complete is not the ending itself. It is the path that runs after one of those finish routes fires, so keep critical clues before the finish point. Keep one deliberate finish path. A final objective, a closing message and then Complete quest is the easiest shape to read. Check it in a dry run before export. ← Your first quest Node reference → HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-09-18."},{"url":"export.html#export-dialog","title":"The export window","section":"export","text":"Opening the export window shows you what you are about to ship before it is packed. Its own words for the job: Compiles everything into a ready-to-play mod folder — no coding needed. Unzip it into the game's mods/ directory. Quests Every quest in the project, by title. If a quest is missing from this list, it is not in the mod either. Websites Every host your mod registers. Worth a look, because domains belong to the whole game and two mods claiming one host will fight over it. Permissions requested The list below. The game shows these to the player when they install, so a shorter list is a friendlier one. In the zip Every file that will be packed, by path. Nothing is hidden from you here. N"},{"url":"export.html#permissions","title":"The seven permissions","section":"export","text":"A permission is a promise about what your mod touches. You do not pick them anywhere: the editor works out which ones your nodes need and asks for exactly those. Delete the node and the permission goes with it. Permission Asked for by What it lets the mod do network Create network · Register domain · Create database · Place files Put machines, names and files into the game's world. filesystem Place files Write files onto the player's own machine. shell Run terminal command · Tool response · Manual input Run commands, answer for the game's tools, and add a command of your own. events When event · Claim another quest Watch for what the player does, and start another quest. mail Dialogue, with "},{"url":"export.html#zip-contents","title":"What is in the .zip","section":"export","text":"Two of these files are the mod. The rest are there so that somebody who wants to change the mod later can rebuild it, and so the folder makes sense to anyone who opens it. You never need to touch any of them. File What it is dist/mod.js The mod. Everything you built, turned into something the game can run. manifest.json The mod's card: its name, author, description, version and the permissions it asks for. This is what the game reads first. dist/manifest.json A second copy of the card, where the game also looks for it. README.md A short description of the mod, with its permissions and any warnings the export produced. src/index.ts The same mod as dist/mod.js , in the form a developer would e"},{"url":"export.html#installing","title":"Installing it","section":"export","text":"The file downloads with a name built from your mod's identifier and version, so meridian-1.0.0.zip is version 1.0.0 of the mod called meridian . Two versions of one mod therefore never share a filename, which matters when you are testing a change against the last one. Open the export window and read the report. Fix anything red. Choose Download .zip . The button reads Packing… while the file is built. Unzip the folder into the game's mods/ directory, as the export window tells you. The folder inside the archive is the mod; the archive itself is not. Start the game. The mod is loaded, and its quests are available to accept. Unzip it, do not drop it in whole. The game reads a folder in mods/ ,"},{"url":"export.html#export-is-not-a-save","title":"Export is not a save","section":"export","text":"This is the mistake worth understanding properly, because it loses work. The .zip is the finished mod. It is the thing the game plays, and it is not something you can open in the editor and carry on editing. Your project file is the editable thing. It holds your canvas, your node settings and your websites in the form the editor understands. Saving keeps that up to date; exporting only produces a copy for the game to run. To keep working Save the project, and open the project again next time. Not the .zip . To ship a change Edit the project, then export again. The new .zip replaces the old folder in mods/ . If you only have the .zip The quests inside it still play, but the canvas you built t"},{"url":"export.html#build-stamp","title":"The build stamp","section":"export","text":"Every export writes the editor's own build into dist/mod.js , as 2026-09-18.r188 . It is how you tell which version of the editor produced a mod, which is the first thing worth knowing when a quest behaves differently for someone else than it does for you. The same stamp is in the footer of every page of this handbook, so you can check that the handbook you are reading describes the editor you are using. A handbook for an older build can describe a field that has since moved. When you report a problem, give the build. Open any .zip you exported and the stamp is at the top of dist/mod.js . It saves everyone a round of questions. ← Checking your quest When something goes wrong → ↑ back to top "},{"url":"guides.html#inspector-tabs","title":"The inspector's three tabs","section":"guides","text":"The panel on the right holds three tabs, and one of them is disabled until you need it. Node Every field on the node you have selected. Greyed out when nothing is selected, because there is nothing to show. Quest Settings for the quest you are looking at. This is the default tab, so it is what you see on an empty canvas. Mod Settings for the whole file: the name on the box, the version, the icon. The inspector at rest. Node waits for a selection. The panel can also leave the side of the window. Drag its left edge outward and it floats, which helps when a node sits underneath it. Drag the title bar to move it and the bottom-right corner to resize it. The same panel, floating. Its position is "},{"url":"guides.html#quest-settings","title":"Quest settings","section":"guides","text":"Five groups, thirteen settings. Most quests need four of them. Identity Quest identifier Internal. Unique across your mod, and the thing other quests name when they start this one. Display title What the player sees in their quest list. Description The pitch, shown before they accept. This is also the only place to say which addons your quest needs. Journal group Folders the player's journal into groups. Two quests in the same group read as one story. Rewards Money and XP , both plain numbers. These are what the game pays for finishing the quest, which is separate from anything a Pay the player node does mid-story. Behaviour Start automatically The quest begins on its own instead of waiting "},{"url":"guides.html#mod-settings","title":"Mod settings","section":"guides","text":"These describe the file, not the story. Everything here ends up on the card the game shows the player. Mod id Internal, and part of the exported file's name. Changing it later makes the game treat the result as a different mod. Display name The name on the box. Version Also part of the filename, which is how two builds of one mod avoid overwriting each other. Author Your name. Defaults to the editor's name if you leave it. Description The blurb in the mod list. Cover image and Icon — the two pictures. Both optional, both shipped inside the .zip . Tags Search terms for the mod list. The Mod tab. Permissions appear here but are not editable — your nodes choose them."},{"url":"guides.html#dialogues","title":"Dialogues","section":"guides","text":"The Dialogues button opens the phone conversations, in one place rather than scattered across the canvas. This is where you write who says what, in what order, and with what delay. One conversation, line by line. A conversation needs something to reach it. Nothing wiring into a Dialogue node is a problem the export report will raise, because a conversation nobody triggers is a conversation the player never sees."},{"url":"guides.html#websites","title":"Websites","section":"guides","text":"The Websites button opens the builder: your sites on the left, and the pages of whichever site you picked on the right. A site is a host; a page is a path on it. Sites and their pages. Each page has a live preview beside the editor, so you see roughly what the player will see. Editing one page, preview alongside. A host belongs to the whole game. Two mods claiming one domain collide, and the export report says so. A page can also be unlisted, which means only a direct address or a brute-force tool finds it. That is deliberate when you want a page hidden, and a mistake when you do not."},{"url":"guides.html#templates","title":"Templates","section":"guides","text":"Thirteen starter projects, from a blank canvas to a 36-node quest, each with a difficulty and a node count. The window also imports a project file somebody else saved, which is how quests get shared. The template list. Loading a template replaces what is on the canvas. That is why the editor offers Save current quest first before clearing anything. Two of the thirteen are not quests at all. Node Reference holds one of every node with example input, and Quest Cookbook collects common patterns. Neither is meant to ship — open them to read how something is done, then copy it into your own project."},{"url":"guides.html#dry-run","title":"Dry run","section":"guides","text":"The Dry run button walks through your quest the way the exported mod would run it, without installing anything. It is the closest thing to a test drive the editor has. A trace in progress. Nodes light up as the run reaches them. Watch the order rather than the outcome. If a node you expected never appears, nothing is wired to reach it. Drop a Debug probe after it and run again: the probe prints what a node does when it fires, and whether it fires at all. Dry run is not the game. It runs your logic, not the game's tools. A conversation, a scan or an exploit can still behave differently in the real thing, which is why the timed-conversation warning tells you to check in-game before you ship."},{"url":"guides.html#addons","title":"Addons","section":"guides","text":"Addons are packs made by other people that add events and tool actions to your editor. The window's own description of them: Community addons drop in here as data: their events join the trigger picker, their quest data becomes nodes. Addons are data only — the editor never runs code from them. The addons window. Drag a pack in, and its events and nodes appear. Once a pack is loaded, its events show up in the trigger picker under Community addons , and its tool actions appear in the palette under the same name. Nothing changes until you load one. Your players need the pack too. A quest built on an addon does nothing for anyone without it, and the game will not tell them what is missing. Name "},{"url":"guides.html#settings","title":"Settings","section":"guides","text":"The Settings button holds editor preferences. None of them reach your quest or the exported mod; they change how the editor looks and feels for you. Canvas Show grid , Grid colour , Grid opacity , Grid scale and Snap to grid . Wires Animated wires , Springy wires , Line weight and Mark size . Appearance Interface font , with a Follow the editor theme option for the grid. Getting out Clear the autosaved draft and Reset all editor preferences . Both are deliberate and both ask first. Preferences only. Your quest is untouched by anything here."},{"url":"guides.html#canvas-tools","title":"Canvas tools","section":"guides","text":"Top left of the canvas. These move nodes around; none of them change what your quest does. Tidy up Arranges the whole graph left to right, by how far each node sits from its entry point. The one to reach for after a long building session. Align in a row · Align in a column Puts the selected nodes on one line, centred. Space out across · Space out down Equalises the gaps without moving anything onto a line. The outermost nodes stay where they are. Springy wires Switches the wires between settling and drifting. Purely cosmetic, and off is calmer on a big map. Debug Opens the editor's own diagnostics: the build stamp, wire numbers, counters and a recent-event log. This is not the Debug probe no"},{"url":"guides.html#generate-tags","title":"Two buttons beside a text field","section":"guides","text":"Some text fields carry one or two small buttons. They look alike and do opposite things, which is worth getting straight early. The dice Fills the field with a realistic value it makes up — a plausible address, a named contractor, an e-mail. The result is fixed forever, because it is now text you typed. The sparkle Inserts a tag the game fills in when the quest runs, like the address the game allocates to your network. The value is different every playthrough, because it does not exist yet. Roll the dice when you want something concrete to look at while you build. Use the sparkle when the game is the only thing that knows the answer — a machine address is the common case, since the game allo"},{"url":"how-do-i.html","title":"How do I… — HackHub Quest Editor handbook How do I… Walkthroughs, each one solvi","section":"how-do-i.html","text":"How do I… — HackHub Quest Editor handbook How do I… Walkthroughs, each one solving a single thing an author actually wants. One is written; the other fifteen are listed at the bottom as they are planned. These are not a feature of the editor. They are arrangements other authors have built, written out as steps you can follow. Each one names the nodes it uses, how long it takes, why the arrangement works, and the two or three ways it usually goes wrong. They are drawn from the built-in templates and from how the game's own quests are put together. Being written. This page is scaffolding from th"},{"url":"index.html#contents-learn","title":"Learn it once","section":"index","text":"Your first quest Twenty minutes, start to finish, ending in a mod you install and play. How a quest works The mental model in eight short pieces the rest of the book keeps referring back to."},{"url":"index.html#contents-look","title":"Look things up","section":"index","text":"Node reference One page per node, every setting with its default, limits and what happens when you leave it empty. Grouped the way the palette groups them: Quest lifecycle Quest start · On start & reload · On quest complete · On quest abandoned Objectives Objective Triggers When event World building Create network · Create Wi-Fi · Firewall rule · Change port · Register domain · Create database · Place files · Tool response Communication Dialogue Custom terminal Manual input Effects Pay the player · Charge the player · Notify · Ask player · Set quest data · Claim another quest · Complete quest · Retire quest · Unclaim quest · Run terminal command · Open handbook Community addons Give data to "},{"url":"index.html#contents-fix","title":"When it goes sideways","section":"index","text":"Checking your quest Every diagnostic message the editor can show you — canvas, inspector, panels and the export report — searchable by pasting the exact text. When something goes wrong A decision tree that starts from the symptom, not the cause. Exporting and installing What the export window checks, what the zip holds, where it goes, and why export is not a save. Glossary and limits Every word, every key, and everything this editor cannot do yet. Three things worth knowing before you start A quest finishes when your graph says so. Put Complete quest at a success ending, or use Behaviour when objectives alone should finish it. Your editor settings are not part of the mod. Theme, font, grid a"},{"url":"nodes/comms-dialogue.html","title":"Dialogue — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Dialogue Communication comms.dialogue W","section":"nodes/comms-dialogue.html","text":"Dialogue — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Dialogue Communication comms.dialogue What it does A conversation between the player and a character: a phone call, a Kisscord chat, an e-mail or a WeeChat session. When you'd use it Use it for anything a character says to the player. Pick the form the story wants: a call for urgency, mail for a paper trail. For phone calls, choose when Out fires : when the call hangs up, or right after it starts. The second output is there for conversations that can go badly. Wire it if the player can answer wrongly. In the game, this looks like The conversation appear"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-accountId","title":"Account","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Which of the mod's Twotter accounts posts these. Accounts are shared by every quest, so one character can carry a story arc across several of them — make one with “Manage accounts”. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-tweets","title":"Tweets","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"One row is a single post. A handful of rows with “Already on the profile” times is a history the player finds when they look the character up — a month of excitement before a disappearance, say. Default a starter list The button reads Add a tweet ."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-content","title":"Tweet","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"What the character wrote. Tags work here, like everywhere else an author writes text."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-timeMode","title":"When","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"“When the story arrives” posts the tweet as the flow reaches this node. “Already on the profile” has it sitting there from the start, that far back. Limits One of the 2 listed below. Choice What it is stored as When the story arrives arrival Already on the profile earlier"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-agoAmount","title":"How long ago","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Counted back from the moment the story reaches this node, on the in-game clock. Limits 1 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-agoUnit","title":"Unit","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Minutes through years, the same units a Timer takes. Limits One of the 6 listed below. Choice What it is stored as minutes minutes hours hours days days weeks weeks months months years years"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-likes","title":"Likes","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Cosmetic, but it sells the fiction. A brand-new account with 400 likes reads as fake. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-comments","title":"Replies","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many replies the post shows. The game does not open them — this is the number under the post, nothing more. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-shares","title":"Reposts","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many times the post was shared. Zero is fine and often truest — a small account screaming into the void. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-views","title":"Views","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many people saw it. It has to look plausible next to the likes: thousands of views with two likes reads wrong. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-showInTimeline","title":"Also in the main timeline","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Off keeps a year of history on the profile where the player looked for it. On drops it into the public feed too — good for an announcement the whole city should see. Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the tweets are posted. Out output Whatever happens after the posts go out. The node does not wait for the player to read anything. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by"},{"url":"nodes/entry-abandon.html","title":"On quest abandoned — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest abandoned Quest life","section":"nodes/entry-abandon.html","text":"On quest abandoned — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest abandoned Quest lifecycle entry.abandon What it does Runs if the player abandons the quest. Use it to tidy up whatever the quest left behind. When you'd use it Reach for it when your quest changes the world in a way that should not outlive a quit: a machine left unlocked, a contact left waiting mid-conversation. Most quests do not need it. If nothing has to be undone, delete the node rather than leaving it sitting there empty. In the game, this looks like The player drops the quest and this fires. Anything you wired after it runs once"},{"url":"nodes/entry-complete.html","title":"On quest complete — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest complete Quest lifecy","section":"nodes/entry-complete.html","text":"On quest complete — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest complete Quest lifecycle entry.complete What it does Runs when the quest is marked complete. That can come from a Complete quest node, auto-complete, or the player's Complete button. When you'd use it Use it for cleanup or epilogue beats that should happen after the quest is finished. For a deliberate ending, place Complete quest where the story should finish. Anything wired here runs after that finish point, not before it. In the game, this looks like The player sees the quest finish, then this path runs through the game's quest-compl"},{"url":"nodes/entry-load.html","title":"On start & reload — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On start & reload Quest lifecy","section":"nodes/entry-load.html","text":"On start & reload — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On start & reload Quest lifecycle entry.load What it does Runs when the quest is claimed, and again every time the game loads it. Anything that has to be true in every session goes here. When you'd use it Put anything that watches for a player action here rather than in Quest start . A watcher armed once stops working after the player reloads. Use it for the pieces a returning player expects to still be working: the listeners behind your triggers, the state of a machine they left mid-job. Keep one-off story beats out of it. They belong in Quest"},{"url":"nodes/entry-start.html","title":"Quest start — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Quest start Quest lifecycle entry.st","section":"nodes/entry-start.html","text":"Quest start — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Quest start Quest lifecycle entry.start What it does Runs the moment the quest is claimed. Anything the story needs in place before the player acts goes here. When you'd use it This is the front door of your quest. Wire the opening to its Out socket: the briefing mail, the first objective, whatever the player should meet first. You can have more than one, and the game runs all of them when the quest is claimed. Splitting a long opening across two is fine. It has no input socket, because nothing runs before it. In the game, this looks like The player "},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-title","title":"Headline","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"A short name for this beat, e.g. “Act 2 — the firewall”. It is the card's title bar. What to put here The card's title bar. Name the beat after the scene. Example Act 2 — the firewall Default empty If you leave it empty The card has no title, so on a crowded canvas you cannot tell one beat from another."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-text","title":"Beat text","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"The key content of the beat. The card previews the top ~800 characters and expands into a scrollable panel for the rest. What to put here What happens in the beat. The card previews the top of it and expands in the inspector. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-choices","title":"Branch choices","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Add a row for each path the story can take. Each gets its own output socket on the card, so you can wire each branch onward while planning. Default an empty list The button reads Add choice ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-label","title":"Choice","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Short title for this path, e.g. “Go in through the front door”. Example Go in through the front door"},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-note","title":"What happens","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"One line on where this path leads, so a branching beat reads at a glance."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-width","title":"Width (px)","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"How wide the card is on the canvas, in pixels. Default 280 Limits 200 to 640. Whole numbers, in steps of 20."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-color","title":"Card colour","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Pick any colour. Colour-coding by act, character or branch is usually the clearest. What to put here Colour the card by act, character or branch, so a big canvas reads at a glance. Default \"#64748b\" Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Story Beat . Common mistakes Expecting"},{"url":"nodes/flow-branch.html#node-flow-branch-field-source","title":"Test against","section":"nodes/flow-branch","text":"Test against details from the event that fired, or against quest data you stored earlier with a “Set quest data” node. What to put here Pick what the condition is tested against. <b class=\"ui\">Event</b> tests the details carried by the event that fired. <b class=\"ui\">Data</b> tests values you stored with <a href=\"fx-setdata.html\">Set quest data</a>. Default \"event\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Pick <b class=\"ui\">Data</b> when the thing you are testing happened earlier in the quest. The event that woke this branch only carries its own details. Choice What it is stored as Details from the event event Quest data data"},{"url":"nodes/flow-branch.html#node-flow-branch-field-conditions","title":"Take the “Yes” path when","section":"nodes/flow-branch","text":"All clauses must hold for the “Yes” path. Otherwise the “No” path runs. What to put here Add one clause per thing that has to be true. Default an empty list If you leave it empty With no clauses there is nothing to fail, so the <b class=\"ui\">Yes</b> path always runs and the <b class=\"ui\">No</b> path never does. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the split. Trigger input The event that wakes the branch, when it should react to something the player does. Yes output The path that runs when every clause holds. No output The path that runs when any clause fails. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a valu"},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-label","title":"Label","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Names itself after whatever you wire it to — socket, node and detail. Type your own to override it. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-includeData","title":"Include saved values","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Print everything the quest has saved with a “Remember a value” node. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-includePayload","title":"Include the event","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Also print the details the game passed to this point, so you can see the real values instead of guessing what arrived. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-toast","title":"Show on screen too","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Also show it on screen, so you can test without reading the log file. Default off Limits On or off. Watch out Switch it off before you ship. Debug output a player can see shows them the seams. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find Debug probe under "},{"url":"nodes/flow-delay.html#node-flow-delay-field-seconds","title":"Seconds","section":"nodes/flow-delay","text":"How long the story pauses here. Fractions are fine — 0.5 waits half a second. Example 2 Default 1 Limits 0 or more. Steps of 0.5, so fractions that fine are allowed. If you leave it empty There is no pause. A blank value counts as zero and the story carries straight on. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them a"},{"url":"nodes/flow-note.html#node-flow-note-field-text","title":"Note","section":"nodes/flow-note","text":"Shown on the canvas only. Notes are never exported into the mod. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-note.html#node-flow-note-field-width","title":"Width","section":"nodes/flow-note","text":"How wide the note is on the canvas, in pixels. Default 240 Limits 160 to 640. Whole numbers, in steps of 20. Wires This node has no sockets of its own. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Sticky note . Related nodes Group frame — A named box that moves its nodes. Story Beat — Plans a story beat. Never runs. ← Debug probe Story Beat → ↑ back to top HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-09-18.r188 · fiel"},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-options","title":"Options","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"One of these is picked at random and stored. Add as many as you like. What to put here The values to choose between. Add as many as you like. Default an empty list Watch out With one option there is nothing random about it. With none, there is nothing to pick. The button reads Add option ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-label","title":"Value","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"The value stored if this option is picked."},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-storeAs","title":"Store the result as","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"A name you choose. The picked value is saved under it so you can read it back later with {{data.name}}. Example guardOnDuty If you leave it empty The pick is not stored, so nothing can read it back later. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Random pick . Common mistakes On"},{"url":"nodes/flow-reroute.html","title":"Reroute — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Reroute Flow control flow.reroute What i","section":"nodes/flow-reroute.html","text":"Reroute — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Reroute Flow control flow.reroute What it does A point to route wires through. It tidies the canvas and changes nothing about the story. When you'd use it Use it when one node has to feed several others, or when a wire would otherwise cross half the canvas. Double-click any wire on the canvas to drop one in automatically. In the game, this looks like Nothing. It changes how the canvas looks, not how the quest runs. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in the story and runs whatever is wired after it. Wires Socket Direction What to"},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-steps","title":"Outputs, in order","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"They fire top to bottom. Drag the rows' ✕ to remove an output — any wire attached to it is removed too. What to put here One row per output, in the order they should fire. Default a starter list Watch out Removing a row removes its socket, and any wire attached to it goes with it. The button reads Add output ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-label","title":"Name","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"Free text — whatever helps you recognise this output on the canvas, e.g. “lights out” or “call Mara”. What to put here A name for the output, so you can tell the sockets apart on the canvas."},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-delayMs","title":"Wait before firing (milliseconds)","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"How long to pause before this output fires, counted from the previous one. 0 fires it immediately; 1000 is one second. Example 1500 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 100. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Sequence . Common mistakes A row is deleted without checking its wire. The socket goes with it and whatever hun"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-mode","title":"When it fires","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"A stretch to wait, a clock time counted from now, or a fixed moment in the story's own calendar. Default \"after\" Limits One of the 3 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Wait after A coming day daytime An exact date at"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-years","title":"Years","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"Whole in-game years to wait, before the shorter units beside it. A target month with fewer days uses its last day. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. A whole in-game year added to the calendar — the day number is kept, and a leap day that does not exist next year falls back to 28 February."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-months","title":"Months","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"Whole in-game months to wait, on top of the years. 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not in March. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. A whole in-game month added to the calendar: 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not in March, because February has no 31st."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-weeks","title":"Weeks","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game weeks to wait, on top of the months. One week is seven in-game days. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. A week is seven in-game days, so 2 weeks and 14 days wait exactly the same length of time."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-days","title":"Days","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game days to wait first. 0 is fine — the units are added up. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. Every unit is added up, so days, months and hours all count towards one due time — blank everywhere means nothing waits."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-hours","title":"Hours","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game hours, on top of the days. 25 hours is a legal value. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. The units are added up, so 25 hours is a legal value."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-minutes","title":"Minutes","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game minutes, on top of the days and hours. At the default game speed one in-game minute passes every real second. Example 30 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. These are in-game minutes: at the default game speed one in-game minute passes every real second, so 30 minutes is half a real minute."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetYears","title":"Years","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game years from now, counted from the day the story reaches this node. A target month with fewer days uses its last day. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no years. Counted from the in-game day the player reaches this node, so “1 year, at 18:23” is that same date next year; a 29 February falls back to 28 February when the next year has no leap day."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetMonths","title":"Months","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game months from now, on top of the years. 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not in March. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no months. Whole in-game months, counted from the day the player gets here: 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February rather than skipping into March."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetWeeks","title":"Weeks","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game weeks from now, on top of the months. One week is seven in-game days. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no weeks. A week is seven in-game days, so 2 weeks is 14 days — the clock time below is what the timer rings at, whatever time the player arrived."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetDays","title":"Days","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game days from now, on top of the weeks and months. 0 means today, so “0 days, at 09:00” fires the next time the clock reads 09:00. Example 3 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means today — the next time the clock reads the time below. Days are added after the months and weeks, and 0 with 0 in the other boxes is a legal “today at 09:00”."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateDay","title":"Day","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game day of the month, as the in-game clock shows it. The field warns when that day does not exist. Example 20 Default 0 Limits 1 to 31. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 1–31, as the in-game clock shows it."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateMonth","title":"Month","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game month, by name, as the in-game clock shows it. Example 9 Default 0 Limits One of the 13 listed below. If you leave it empty 1–12, as the in-game clock shows it. January is 1. Choice What it is stored as Not set yet 0 January 1 February 2 March 3 April 4 May 5 June 6 July 7 August 8 September 9 October 10 November 11 December 12"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateYear","title":"Year","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game year, e.g. 2026. Read it off the in-game clock, not your own calendar. Example 2026 Default 0 Limits 1970 to 9999. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Read it off the in-game clock, not your own calendar. With no full date set, the timer fires the moment the story reaches it."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-hour","title":"Hour","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The hour the in-game clock will show, 0–23. This is the player's clock, not the wall clock. Example 12 Default 0 Limits 0 to 23. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 is legal — it is 00:00, the middle of the in-game night. The time the in-game clock will show, not the wall clock."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-minute","title":"Minute","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The minute the in-game clock will show, 0–59. Example 0 Default 0 Limits 0 to 59. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 is legal — on the hour. In-game seconds are real-world milliseconds, so there is no second field. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Tim"},{"url":"nodes/fx-claimquest.html#node-fx-claimquest-field-questName","title":"Quest","section":"nodes/fx-claimquest","text":"The identifier of the quest to start next. It must exist in this mod or another installed one. What to put here The identifier of the quest to start next, exactly as that quest names itself. Example meridian-act-two Default empty If you leave it empty There is no quest to claim, so the node does nothing. Watch out The identifier has to match. A name that exists nowhere claims nothing, and the player is left with a finished story and no next chapter. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire"},{"url":"nodes/fx-completequest.html","title":"Complete quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Complete quest Effects fx.complet","section":"nodes/fx-completequest.html","text":"Complete quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Complete quest Effects fx.completeQuest What it does Marks this quest complete. Rewards can pay out, and On quest complete runs next. When you'd use it Use it as the final node of a successful story path. Put closing mail, payments and important clues before it, unless they belong under On quest complete . It has no output because the current story path ends here. In the game, this looks like The quest leaves the active list as completed. Quest rewards and complete-hook work can run. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in "},{"url":"nodes/fx-handbook.html#node-fx-handbook-field-articleId","title":"Article","section":"nodes/fx-handbook","text":"The in-game article the player lands on. Pick a known page, or type any article id. Watch out An identifier that matches no article opens nothing, and the player gets no explanation of why."},{"url":"nodes/fx-handbook.html#node-fx-handbook-field-category","title":"Category","section":"nodes/fx-handbook","text":"The handbook section the article sits under. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Open handbook . Common mistakes The article belongs to another mod. The player does not have it, so the handbook opens on nothing. Point at an article your own mod ships. Messages you might se"},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-message","title":"Message","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"The text on the popup. Type {{data.name}} to insert a value you saved earlier. What to put here One or two sentences is plenty. It sits over the game, not in a journal. Example Payment received: Meridian Capital. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The popup appears with nothing in it. The player sees an empty box and no idea why."},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-variant","title":"Style","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"A notification is a persistent popup; a toast slides in and fades on its own. What to put here Pick how insistent the message should be. Default \"notify\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out A notification waits for the player to close it. Use a toast for anything they can afford to miss. Choice What it is stored as Notification popup notify Toast toast"},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-tone","title":"Tone","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"Sets the colour and icon: info, success, warning or error. What to put here Match it to what the message means: a success for a payment, a warning for a mistake. Default \"info\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Info info Success success Warning warning Error error Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection "},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-amount","title":"Amount","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"Credits deposited into the player's bank account. What to put here The dollars to award when this node runs. Example 250 Default 100 Limits 0 or more. If you leave it empty A zero payout still runs, but the player receives nothing."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-description","title":"Description","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The label on the bank statement line. What to put here Write how the client names the job when the transfer appears. Example Meridian Capital — contract settlement Default \"Job payment\" If you leave it empty The money arrives with no useful label, so the reward looks unexplained."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-fromIBAN","title":"From IBAN","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The sending account, shown in the transfer details. What to put here Use this when a client, shell company or agency should appear as the sender. Example BG80BNBG96611020345678 If you leave it empty The game still creates a transfer. Fill it when the sender matters to the fiction."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-fromName","title":"From name","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The sender's name on the statement. What to put here Name the sender shown in the transfer details. Example Meridian Capital If you leave it empty The payment still arrives. Fill it when the player should recognise who paid them. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever moment earns the money. Out output The closing message, next objective, or follow-up effect after the payment lands. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step "},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-title","title":"Title","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The heading at the top of the question box. Leave blank if the question itself is enough. Example Security check Default \"Answer needed\" Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The box can still read well if the question says enough on its own."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-label","title":"Question","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The sentence above the answer box — what you want the player to answer. Example Enter the recovery code: Default \"Type your answer:\" Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The player sees an input box with little context. Usually this should ask the actual question."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-placeholder","title":"Example text","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Grey hint inside an empty answer box. It disappears as soon as the player types. What to put here A sample shape helps when the answer has dashes, spaces or a special format. Example ABCD-1234 Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty Nothing grey appears inside the empty box. That is fine for plain questions."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-defaultValue","title":"Starts filled with","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Text already in the answer box when it opens. Leave blank when the player should type from scratch. What to put here Use this when editing an answer is faster than typing it from scratch. Example MSKU- Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The answer box starts empty. That is the normal choice."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-password","title":"Mask typing","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Hides what the player types, like a password box. Use for passphrases, keys and private codes. What to put here Turn it on for secrets the player should not see echoed back on screen. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-storeAs","title":"Save answer as","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Optional name for the answer. If you type playerAnswer here, later nodes can read it with {{data.playerAnswer}}. Leave blank if only this choice matters. What to put here Use a short name such as verdict, handle or recoveryCode. Example recoveryCode Default empty If you leave it empty The answer is not kept after this node. That is fine when the outgoing wire is all you need. Watch out If later text says {{data.recoveryCode}}, this field must say recoveryCode."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-matchMode","title":"Accept","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Choose whether any submitted text continues, or whether the answer must match something you set. What to put here Pick Any submitted text for open questions. Pick one of the checked choices when a specific answer should unlock the next beat. Default \"any\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Watch out Once you choose a checked answer, the node grows a Wrong output. Wire it if a miss should do more than stop this beat. Choice What it is stored as Any submitted text any Exactly this answer exact Contains these words contains Matches a pattern regex"},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-expected","title":"Answer to accept","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The text that counts as the right answer. Tags are filled before the check, so a saved password can be accepted too. Example ABCD-1234 Default empty When it appears Shown only when Accept is Exactly this answer, Contains these words or Matches a pattern . Until then the field is hidden. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty Every typed answer misses. Fill this in, or switch Accept back to Any submitted text. Watch out The accepted answer needs a clue somewhere in the quest. Without that, the player is guessing."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-caseSensitive","title":"Case sensitive","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Turn off to accept any capitalisation. Turn on only when upper and lower case are part of the puzzle. Default off Limits On or off. When it appears Shown only when Accept is Exactly this answer, Contains these words or Matches a pattern . Until then the field is hidden. Watch out Left on, apple and Apple are different answers. Keep it off unless capitals matter. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should happen right before the question appears. Submitted output The path after a submitted answer. When Accept checks for a specific answer, this is the Correct path. Wrong output The path for a submitted answer that does not match. This socket appears only after Accept check"},{"url":"nodes/fx-retirequest.html","title":"Retire quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Retire quest Effects fx.retireQuest","section":"nodes/fx-retirequest.html","text":"Retire quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Retire quest Effects fx.retireQuest What it does Takes this quest out of the player's active list without marking it complete. When you'd use it Use it for fail states, cancelled jobs, or stories that should vanish without rewards. Do not use it for a normal success ending. Use Complete quest for that. It has no output because the current story path ends here. In the game, this looks like The quest disappears from the active list. It does not count as completed. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in the story and runs whate"},{"url":"nodes/fx-setdata.html#node-fx-setdata-field-key","title":"Key","section":"nodes/fx-setdata","text":"A name you choose, like doorCode. Use the same name later to read this value back. Example doorCode Default empty If you leave it empty The value is stored under no name, so nothing can find it again."},{"url":"nodes/fx-setdata.html#node-fx-setdata-field-value","title":"Value","section":"nodes/fx-setdata","text":"Any text is fine — a word, a number, an address. It is just stored, so nothing here can error. You can also insert a live value by typing {{data.name}} for something you saved earlier. Example 4417 Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . Watch out The name has to match exactly when you read it back. A typo reads as an empty value and nothing warns you. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same "},{"url":"nodes/fx-shell.html#node-fx-shell-field-command","title":"Command","section":"nodes/fx-shell","text":"The command executed in the player's terminal, as if they had typed it. Tags like {{data.targetIp}} are filled in first. What to put here The command to run. Tags work here, so you can drop in a value you stored earlier. Example nmap {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty There is nothing to run, so the node does nothing. Watch out It runs whether or not the player is ready for it. A command that needs an earlier step will fail in front of them. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this nod"},{"url":"nodes/fx-unclaimquest.html#node-fx-unclaimquest-field-questName","title":"Quest","section":"nodes/fx-unclaimquest","text":"The identifier of the claimed quest to remove. Leave blank to remove this quest. What to put here Copy the internal name from the quest strip or the other mod notes. Blank targets this quest. Example side-contract Default empty If you leave it empty The current quest is removed. Watch out A spelling mismatch removes nothing, so the unwanted quest stays active. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input The point where the chosen quest should be removed. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and"},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-amountMode","title":"Amount type","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"A fixed sum, or a slice of whatever the player currently has in the bank. What to put here Pick which of the two numbers below this node reads. <b class=\"ui\">Fixed amount</b> reads <b class=\"ui\">Amount</b>. <b class=\"ui\">Percentage of balance</b> reads <b class=\"ui\">Percent</b>. Default \"fixed\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Both number fields stay visible whichever you pick, and the one you did not pick is ignored. Check this first when a charge comes out wrong. Choice What it is stored as Fixed amount fixed Percentage of balance percent"},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-amount","title":"Amount","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"Credits taken from the player's account. What to put here The sum to take, in dollars. Example 35 Default 100 Limits 0 or more. If you leave it empty Nothing happens. The charge is skipped with no warning and no line in the bank statement."},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-percent","title":"Percent","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"Percentage of the player's current balance, taken when this node runs. What to put here The share of the player's current balance to take. 10 takes a tenth of whatever they have right now. Default 10 Limits 0 to 100. Watch out The result is rounded to a whole number, so a small balance can produce a charge of zero."},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-description","title":"Description","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"The label on the bank statement line. Example Tool licence — packet sniffer Default \"Purchase\" Limits Technically any text, but keep it short. A statement line is only a few words. If you leave it empty The statement line has no label, so the player sees money vanish from nowhere. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mism"},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-label","title":"Name","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"Shown in the frame's title bar — name the cluster after what it does, e.g. “Act 1: recon”. Default \"Group\""},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-color","title":"Title bar colour","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"Colour-code your frames however you like — e.g. one colour per act, or per character. It only changes how the frame looks in the editor. Default \"#64748b\""},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-comment","title":"Comment","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"A note to future-you about what this cluster does. What to put here Worth writing as though a stranger will read it, because they might. This text is the part of the frame that survives into the exported mod. Default empty Watch out A comment containing the two characters that close a block comment cannot break the export. The compiler escapes it for you. Wires This node has no sockets of its own. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The insp"},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-name","title":"Identifier","section":"nodes/objective","text":"A short unique name for this objective so other nodes can refer to it. Lowercase with dashes reads well. Example dock-shipment Default empty If you leave it empty Other nodes have nothing to refer to. Name the objective before you wire anything to it."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-description","title":"Shown to the player","section":"nodes/objective","text":"The line shown in the player's quest journal. A clear instruction tends to read well, but any wording that fits your quest works. What to put here Write it as an instruction: what to do, and where. Example Find the shipment manifest on the dock terminal. Default empty Limits Technically any text, but keep it to one line. The journal truncates a long one. If you leave it empty The journal shows a blank task. The player has no idea what they are meant to do."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-hint","title":"Hint","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Revealed only if the player asks for help. As gentle or as cryptic as you want it to be. Example The manifest sits on the machine by the gate, not the office."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-info","title":"Extra info","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Extra detail in the journal's expanded view. Good for lore or background. What to put here Lore belongs here rather than in the journal line. Example Meridian Capital moved the shipment twice after the strike."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-terminalCommand","title":"Suggested command","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Suggested command shown in the journal. It runs nothing — it is a copy-pasteable nudge. Example ssh dock01.meridian.local Watch out It runs nothing on its own. It is a nudge the player can copy, not a shortcut."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-hidden","title":"Hidden until unlocked","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Keep the objective out of the journal until another objective unlocks it. Use for twists. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the objective is handed to the player — usually Quest start or the objective before it. Trigger input The When event node that ticks this objective over. Unlocked by input An objective that has to finish before this one becomes available. On complete output Whatever should happen once the player finishes — the next objective, a payment, the ending. Unlocks output The objective this one opens up. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire o"},{"url":"nodes/pack-node.html","title":"Addon node — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Addon node Community addons pack.node","section":"nodes/pack-node.html","text":"Addon node — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Addon node Community addons pack.node What it does Runs a node supplied by a community addon instead of one built into the editor. When you'd use it Use it when an addon adds something the editor does not have. Addon nodes appear under Editor Mods in the palette. You need the addon installed. Without it the node has nothing to run, and the player misses whatever it was meant to do. In the game, this looks like Whatever the addon's node does, at the point the story reaches it. Fields This node has no fields of its own. Pick an installed addon and one o"},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-commandName","title":"Command name","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The terminal command the player runs, e.g. decrypt. It appears in help output. Example decrypt Default \"decrypt\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-commandDescription","title":"Help text","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The one-line description shown next to the command in help. Example Decrypt the intercepted payload Default \"Decrypt the intercepted payload\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-prompt","title":"Prompt","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The text printed before the cursor, e.g. \"Passphrase >\". Example Passphrase > Default \"Passphrase >\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-mask","title":"Mask the input","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Hides typing behind * — the player sees “Passphrase > ****” instead of their answer, like a password box. Use it when the answer is a secret. What to put here Turn this on to hide what the player types behind asterisks. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-matchMode","title":"Match","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Exactly equals checks the whole answer. Contains accepts it anywhere in what the player types. Matches pattern is for advanced authors who want to accept a whole family of answers at once. What to put here How the answer is checked. Default \"exact\" Limits One of the 3 listed below. Watch out A pattern match is the hardest of the three for a player to guess at. Use it only when several answers should count. Choice What it is stored as Exactly equals exact Contains contains Matches pattern regex"},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-expected","title":"Expected answer","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The answer that counts as correct — or the pattern, if you chose “Matches pattern” above. Default empty If you leave it empty Nothing the player types can be correct, so the story never leaves the wrong path. Watch out The player has to be able to find this somewhere. An answer with no clue behind it is a guess, not a puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-caseSensitive","title":"Case sensitive","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Turn off to accept any capitalisation. Recommended unless case is part of the puzzle. Default off Limits On or off. Watch out Left on, a player who types the right word with the wrong capitals fails. Turn it off unless case is part of the puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-successMessage","title":"Success message","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Printed on a match, then the green “Correct” socket fires. Example Decrypted. Default \"Decrypted.\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-failureMessage","title":"Failure message","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Printed on a miss, then the red “Wrong” socket fires. The player can run the command again. Default \"Wrong passphrase.\" Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the player is asked. Correct output The path that runs when the answer matches. Wrong output The path that runs on a miss. The player can try again, so this is not usually the end. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add thi"},{"url":"nodes/trigger-event.html#node-trigger-event-field-event","title":"Game event","section":"nodes/trigger-event","text":"All supported HackHub events, listed with the details each one carries. Default empty If you leave it empty There is nothing to listen for, so the node never fires."},{"url":"nodes/trigger-event.html#node-trigger-event-field-conditions","title":"Only when","section":"nodes/trigger-event","text":"Leave empty to fire on any occurrence. What to put here Add one clause per thing that has to be true. Default an empty list Watch out The same action can raise more than one event. Check the list for the one that carries the details you need. Wires Socket Direction What to connect When output The objective or branch that should react when the event fires. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find When ev"},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-host","title":"Host IP","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The address the player points a database client at. Example {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-user","title":"Username","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The login sqlmap or a client uses. What to put here The login the player uses. Example admin Default \"admin\""},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-password","title":"Password","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The password. Give the player a way to find it — a config file, a leaked dump, a cracked hash. Default \"secret123\" Watch out The default is a placeholder. Leave it in place and the player can guess their way in without playing."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-tables","title":"Tables","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The data inside: tables holding rows of named values, ready for the player's SQL. Default an empty list If you leave it empty The database exists and holds nothing, so there is nothing to find."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"Drop the database when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Create database . Common mistakes No route to the password. The player finds the database and cannot open it. Hide the password somewhere they can reach. Messages you mi"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-domain","title":"Domain","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The hostname the player types, e.g. vault.corp-internal.net. Example vault.corp-internal.net Default empty If you leave it empty There is no name to resolve, so the node does nothing."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-ip","title":"Resolves to","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The address it resolves to. nslookup and dig will report this. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-vulnerabilities","title":"Vulnerabilities","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"Weaknesses on the host behind this name. In-game scanners report them when the player probes it. What to put here The weaknesses on the host behind this name. A scanner reports the ones you list. Default an empty list The button reads Add vulnerability ."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-type","title":"Type","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The kind of weakness this machine has. The in-game scanners report it when the player probes the machine. What to put here The kind of weakness. Each one says what the player can do with it. Limits One of the 7 listed below. Choice What it is stored as SQL_INJECTION (steal from the database) SQL_INJECTION XSS (script in web pages) XSS CORS (read other sites' data) CORS SSRF (make the server fetch) SSRF LFI (read the server's files) LFI RFI (load a file from your URL) RFI RCE (run any command) RCE"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-version","title":"Version","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The affected component's version, e.g. \"WordPress 5.8\". Cosmetic unless a trigger matches on it. Example WordPress 5.8"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"Drop the DNS entry when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Register domain . Common mistakes The name resolves to an address that does not exist. The player finds the hostname and reaches nothing. Point it at a machine your que"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-target","title":"Where","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Whose filesystem to write to. The player's own machine is written when the flow reaches this node; a remote device is mounted with the machine, under the account the player lands in, so the files are already there when they break in. Default \"player\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Both fields below stay visible whichever you pick, and <b class=\"ui\">Device IP</b> is only read for a remote device. Choice What it is stored as The player's PC player A remote device device"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-ip","title":"Device IP","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Only used for a remote device. Use the same {{data.targetIp}} token you gave the network, and the files are mounted on that machine before the player ever connects. What to put here The remote machine to write to. Only used when <b class=\"ui\">Where</b> says a device. Example {{data.targetIp}} Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-parentPath","title":"Parent folder","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Where the files are mounted. Folders named etc, home, logs or lib are merged into the existing ones rather than replacing them. Example ~/docs Default \"~/\" Watch out Folders named etc, home, logs or lib are merged into the machine's real ones rather than created fresh."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-files","title":"Files","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The files and folders to create. A folder named etc, home, logs or lib merges with the machine's existing one. Default an empty list If you leave it empty Nothing is written. The button reads Add file ."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-name","title":"Name","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The file or folder name, exactly as it appears in ls. Example config"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-extension","title":"Extension","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The extension, e.g. txt, log, conf. Leave blank for folders. Example conf"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-isFolder","title":"Folder","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Mark this entry as a directory rather than a file. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-hidden","title":"Hidden","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Prefix the name with a dot so a plain ls does not show it. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-data","title":"Contents","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The file's contents. This is where clues live — a config file, a log excerpt, a leaked password. Example DB_PASS=hunter2 Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find Place files under World building in the palette. Pick whose machine you are writing to. Se"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-ip","title":"Protected IP","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The machine these rules protect: the quest's own network, or one machine you name. Default \"{{data.targetIp}}\" Limits One of the 1 listed below. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . Choice What it is stored as Random — the quest's network {{data.targetIp}}"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Drop the firewall when the quest ends so the machine is reachable afterwards. Default on Limits On or off. Rule"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-port","title":"Port","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The port this rule applies to. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-allowed","title":"Allowed","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Off means the port is blocked by the firewall. What to put here Turn this on to let traffic through. Off blocks the port. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-source","title":"Source","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Which machines the rule applies to: anywhere on the internet, or one machine you name. Default \"*\" Limits One of the 1 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Anywhere *"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-destination","title":"Destination","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The machine the rule protects — usually the one in “Protected IP” above. Picking it copies that address; changing it later won't follow. Default \"{{data.targetIp}}\""},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-locked","title":"Locked","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The player cannot remove a locked rule. Limits On or off. Watch out A locked rule with no way around it stops the quest. Leave the player another route. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Firewall rule . Common mistakes The rule blocks the only way in. The player cannot f"},{"url":"nodes/world-network.html#node-world-network-field-device","title":"Devices","section":"nodes/world-network","text":"The router at the root of the network, plus everything behind it. Routers and splitters carry children; firewalls carry rules. What to put here The router at the root, and every machine behind it. The editor beside this field is where you add them. Default a starter set Watch out The player needs a route in. If the router exposes nothing and has no weakness, nothing behind it can be reached."},{"url":"nodes/world-network.html#node-world-network-field-destroyOnComplete","title":"Also remove this network when the quest is completed","section":"nodes/world-network","text":"Off by default: the network stays in the world after the quest, the way a real company would. It is also safer, because deleting a machine the player is still connected to can hang the game. Abandoning the quest removes it either way. What to put here Turn this on to take the network back out of the world when the quest completes. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. T"},{"url":"nodes/world-packdata.html","title":"Give data to an addon — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Give data to an addon Comm","section":"nodes/world-packdata.html","text":"Give data to an addon — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Give data to an addon Community addons world.packData What it does Hands a filled-in data shape to a community addon. When you'd use it Use it when an addon your quest depends on asks for data. Pick the pack, pick the shape, fill in what it wants. You need the addon installed to see its fields. Nothing appears here until it is. In the game, this looks like The addon receives the values you entered when the story reaches this node. Fields This node has no fields of its own. Pick an installed addon pack and one of its data shapes, and the edi"},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-ip","title":"Device IP","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"A router IP or any device behind it. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-action","title":"Action","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Open makes an existing port reachable. Close blocks it. Add creates a new service; Remove deletes it entirely. What to put here What to do to the port. Default \"open\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Open open Close close Add add Remove remove"},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.external","title":"External port","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"The port number as seen from outside — what nmap reports. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.internal","title":"Internal port","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"The port the service listens on inside the machine. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.service","title":"Service","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"What nmap prints next to the port, e.g. http, ssh, mysql. Example ssh Default \"ssh\""},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.active","title":"Open","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Turn off to make the port appear closed. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-restoreOnComplete","title":"Restore when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Put the port back the way it was when the quest ends. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Change port . Common mistakes The service name does not match the port number. A scan reporting ssh on port 80 tells the player nobody was paying attenti"},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-command","title":"Tool","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Pick the in-game command this overrides, like nmap or whois. When the player runs it on your input, they see your text instead of the usual result. Default \"nmap\" Limits One of the 11 listed below. Choice What it is stored as nmap nmap hydra hydra whois whois nslookup nslookup mxlookup mxlookup ping ping lynx lynx geoip geoip ssh ssh ftp ftp weechat weechat"},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-input","title":"Keyed by","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"What the player types after the command — the IP, domain or name it answers for. The tool only responds to this exact input. hydra/ssh/ftp use the user + target fields below instead. Example {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty There is nothing to match, so the response never fires."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-inputUser","title":"User","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Only match when the player ran the command against this user."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-inputTarget","title":"Target","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Only match when the player ran the command against this host. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-dataText","title":"Response","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"One “Label: value” per line. The editor turns them into exactly what that tool shows in-game — add a line for each thing you want the player to see. Example PORT: 22 STATE: open SERVICE: ssh Default empty If you leave it empty The command answers with nothing, which reads to the player as a dead host."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Stop intercepting the command when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Tool response . Common mistakes The response answers anything. Key it to a specific input, or the player's first careless scan gives them the answer. The res"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-ssid","title":"Network name (SSID)","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The network name shown in the in-game Wi-Fi list. Example DOCKNET-5G Default \"NEIGHBOUR_5Ghz\" If you leave it empty The access point has no visible name, so the player cannot recognise the right target."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-password","title":"WPA passphrase","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The passphrase the player must discover. Make sure some node in your quest reveals it. Example forklift-1948 Default \"letmein123\" Watch out If the player must type or crack this value later, make sure they can earn it first."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-signal","title":"Signal strength","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The game's Wi-Fi scale: 0 = weakest, 3 = strongest. It also drives how long joining takes. What to put here How strong and quick to join the access point should feel. Default 2 Limits 0 to 3. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-bssid","title":"BSSID","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Optional MAC address shown for the access point. Leave blank to let the game pick one. Example 02:24:00:00:17:48 If you leave it empty The game picks one for you. Fill it only when a clue or test needs a fixed value. Watch out Current game builds may show no network name after Bettercap targets this value, but the attack still works."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-channel","title":"Channel","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Optional 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz channel shown by scans. Leave blank to let the game pick one for the SSID's band. Example 44 Limits 1 to 196. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty The game chooses a channel that fits the network name."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-wps","title":"Advertises WPS","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Whether Wi-Fi scans show WPS for this access point. Leave off unless your story needs to pin it. What to put here Turn it on for targets that should look old, consumer-grade, or poorly locked down. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-model","title":"Router model","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Enables the in-game `fern` recovery route. Leave blank to disable it. Example TP-Link Archer C6 If you leave it empty The access point still exists. Leave this blank if router-model recovery is not part of the puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-users","title":"Router accounts","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Accounts on the access point's own system. Their files land in /home/<username>/. What to put here Accounts on the access point's own router. Use them for an admin-panel step, not for the machine behind it. Default an empty list The button reads Add account ."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-username","title":"Username","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The login name for ssh, ftp or a web login page. Example admin"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-password","title":"Password","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Leave blank to let the game pick one. Example forklift-1948 Default \"letmein123\" Watch out If the player must type or crack this value later, make sure they can earn it first."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-firstName","title":"First name","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Shown on the account's profile page and in whois results."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-lastName","title":"Last name","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Shown on the account's profile page and in whois results."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-emailAddress","title":"E-mail","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The account's e-mail address. Useful as a lead the player can mail. Example admin@docknet.internal"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-acceptReverseTCP","title":"Accepts reverse TCP","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Lets the player open a connection from this account back to their own machine. Turn on only if your quest needs it. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-ports","title":"Router ports","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Open ports on the access point itself. What to put here Services exposed by the access point's router itself. Default an empty list The button reads Add port ."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-external","title":"External port","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The port number as seen from outside. This is what nmap reports and what the player connects to. Example 80 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-internal","title":"Internal port","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The port the service actually listens on inside the machine. Leave equal to the external port unless you are deliberately redirecting. Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-service","title":"Service","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"What nmap prints next to the port, e.g. http, ssh, ftp, mysql. Free text — it is a label, not a real service. Example http"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-version","title":"Version","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The banner nmap -sV prints. Use three numbers and no letters — metasploit refuses \"7.2\" and \"7.2p2\", leaving the player unable to run the exploit. Blank omits the version line. Example nginx 8.4.71"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-active","title":"Open","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Closed ports show as filtered to nmap. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-children","title":"Devices behind the access point","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Machines reachable through this access point. Add a router here to build a second network hop. What to put here The machines the player reaches after joining the Wi-Fi. Default an empty list"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-destroyOnComplete","title":"Also remove this access point when the quest is completed","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Off by default: the access point stays in the world after the quest. Abandoning the quest removes it either way. What to put here Turn this on for a temporary access point that should disappear when the job is done. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should happen before the access point exists. Out output The briefing, objective, or next setup step that assumes the access point is live. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection take"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-entry","title":"Quest lifecycle","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Quest start entry.start Runs once when the quest begins. Setup goes here. On start & reload entry.load Runs on claim and every load. Listeners go here. On quest complete entry.complete Runs after a Complete quest node, auto-complete, or Complete button finishes the quest. On quest abandoned entry.abandon Runs if the player gives up. Cleanup goes here."},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-objective","title":"Objectives","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Objective objective A task the player must complete"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-trigger","title":"Triggers","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does When event trigger.event React to something the player does"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-world","title":"World building","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Create network world.network Routers, devices, firewalls and ports Create Wi-Fi world.wifi A crackable access point Firewall rule world.firewall Allow or block a port Change port world.port Open, close, add or remove a port Register domain world.domain Point a hostname at an IP Create database world.database Content for sqlmap and DatabaseManager Place files world.files Drop files on a PC or a remote device Tool response world.toolResponse Script what a recon tool reports"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-comms","title":"Communication","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Dialogue comms.dialogue A conversation with the player — phone call, Kisscord, e-mail or WeeChat Twotter comms.tweet Post tweets from one of the mod's Twotter accounts — one, or a profile's worth of history"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-reply","title":"Custom terminal","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Manual input reply.input The player types a passphrase"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-effect","title":"Effects","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Pay the player fx.pay Deposit money Charge the player fx.withdraw Withdraw money Notify fx.notify A popup or toast Ask player fx.prompt Ask for one line of text Set quest data fx.setData Store a value for later Claim another quest fx.claimQuest Chain into the next quest Complete quest fx.completeQuest Finish the current quest and run On quest complete Retire quest fx.retireQuest Remove the current quest without marking it complete Unclaim quest fx.unclaimQuest Remove a claimed quest from the player's list Run terminal command fx.shell Execute in the terminal Open handbook fx.handbook Jump to an in-game article"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-community","title":"Community addons","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Give data to an addon world.packData Pick a data shape from an addon and fill it in Addon node pack.node A story node from an addon — find it under Editor Mods"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-flow","title":"Flow control","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Branch flow.branch Split on a condition Wait flow.delay Pause before continuing Timer flow.timer Do something at a later in-game time Reroute flow.reroute Split or tidy a wire Random pick flow.random Choose one option at random Sequence flow.sequence Fire several outputs one after another Debug probe flow.debug Print what is happening here"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-layout","title":"Layout","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Group frame layout.group A named box that moves its nodes Sticky note flow.note A comment on the canvas Story Beat flow.beat Plan a story beat — colour it, preview it ← How a quest works Feature guides → HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-09-18.r188 · every figure on these pages is taken from the editor's own source."},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#start-here","title":"What is happening?","section":"troubleshooting","text":"You see this Go to Nothing at all, in the editor or the game Nothing happens at all An objective stays open forever The objective never ticks The player cannot reach a machine you built The player cannot get in The export window says Needs attention The export report Words on a node you do not recognise Every message, quoted exactly Something else Frequently asked"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-runs","title":"Nothing happens at all","section":"troubleshooting","text":"Work down this list in order. Each step rules out the most common cause first. 1. Nothing is wired to a start A node only runs if a wire reaches it from Quest start or On start & reload . Select your first story node and look at its In socket. If nothing is attached, nothing will ever arrive. 2. The node is unreachable The canvas marks these. A node with an Unreachable badge is on the map but off the story. 3. It is a listener in the wrong place Anything that has to be listening belongs on On start & reload . Set it up on Quest start instead and it is gone the moment the player reloads. 4. You exported but did not unzip The game reads a folder in mods/ , not an archive. A .zip sitting in tha"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#objective-never-ticks","title":"The objective never ticks","section":"troubleshooting","text":"An objective completes one of two ways: the story flow reaches it, or the event wired to its trigger fires. If neither happens, it stays open forever, and the quest cannot be finished. Nothing is wired to its trigger The canvas calls this No trigger , and the quest's Health group counts them. This is the single most common reason a quest cannot be completed. The wire went into the wrong socket In takes a blue flow wire and ticks the objective when the story arrives. Trigger takes a cyan one and waits for the player. A wire into In cannot respond to anything the player does. You wired two events Only the first event wired to a trigger is used. The second is ignored, so if you added a backup, "},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#cannot-get-in","title":"The player cannot get into the machine","section":"troubleshooting","text":"You built a network and the player cannot reach it. Work through the doors in order. The router has no way in The device tree says so in plain words: This router has no way in: set a model for `fern`, or enable support-mail recovery. Neither route is open, so there is nothing to try. The exploit lands in the wrong account An SSH exploit lands in a guest account when it finds one. Your files may be in an account the player has not reached yet. Turn the exploit off for a machine that should hold only the accounts you listed. The address does not exist If a Place files node points at an address nothing uses, the message is red for a reason: the export drops those files rather than failing. Chec"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#export-refuses","title":"The export report","section":"troubleshooting","text":"First, the premise: the export does not refuse. The Download .zip button is disabled only while the file is being packed. It produces a .zip whatever the report says, because a quest with problems still deserves to be tested. What the report is telling you is what will change on the way into the game. Needs attention At least one item is an error. Something you built will be dropped or altered. Fix these before you ship. Good to know Everything exported as you built it, and what you built may not be what you meant. Read them, then decide. The report. It describes consequences; it does not block you. Each item is quoted in full, with its fix, in the report before you export . Paste the wordin"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#faq","title":"Frequently asked","section":"troubleshooting","text":"I set a payment and the player got nothing An amount of 0 , or a blank field, does nothing at all — the node runs and pays nobody. Type a real number. A hundred dollars is a reasonable first fee. I paid a percentage and it came out as zero Percentages are rounded to whole dollars. Ten percent of four dollars is forty cents, which rounds to nothing. Use a fixed amount for small sums. My files are missing in the game Almost always an address mismatch. A Place files node aimed at a network your quest does not create has its files dropped by the export, and the field turns red to say so. The quest panel shows {{data.targetIp}} instead of an address Tags are filled in when the quest starts. Seein"},{"url":"tutorial.html#tour","title":"The screen, labelled","section":"tutorial","text":"Open the editor with nothing loaded and you get a short welcome panel. Its own description of the job: Drag nodes from the left onto the canvas, then wire their sockets together. Every field on the right is a dropdown or a switch — no JSON, no code. That is the whole method, and the screen is arranged around it. The editor with a quest loaded. Five regions, and you use four of them constantly. Top bar The buttons: New , Load , Save , undo, redo, the quest tabs, Dialogues , Websites , Dry run , Addons , Settings and Export mod . Hover any of them for a longer description. Palette Every node the editor can make, grouped by what it does. Type in the Filter nodes box to narrow it. Drag one onto "},{"url":"tutorial.html#new-project","title":"Start from a template","section":"tutorial","text":"Thirteen templates come with the editor, from a blank canvas up to a 36-node quest. You want First Contact : eight nodes, marked Beginner, and deliberately no hacking in it. Its own description: The whole spine in miniature: a brief, one objective (a person lookup), a payment and a closing line. No hacking — just the shape of a quest. Click Browse 13 templates on the welcome panel. If you have already closed that panel, use New instead — though note that New opens a different window that only clears your work. In the window titled Start from a template , choose First Contact . The editor confirms with a message reading Loaded “First Contact”. The template picker. Difficulty and node count ar"},{"url":"tutorial.html#read-the-map","title":"Read the map you loaded","section":"tutorial","text":"Before changing anything, work out what this quest does. Zoom out until all eight nodes fit on screen. Read left to right. The wires, not the positions, decide the order. Follow the blue wires. They read as \"and then\", so a chain of them is the story running in order. You should find a brief being sent to the player, an objective, a payment, and a closing line. That is the spine, and almost every quest you write will be this shape with more in the middle. Now click the objective node. The inspector opens on its fields. The three inspector tabs. Node is the one you will use most. Why this matters. A template you cannot read is a template you cannot edit. Every quest you build later will be a "},{"url":"tutorial.html#add-objective","title":"Add your own objective","section":"tutorial","text":"Now change the quest. You will add a second objective, so the player has two things to do instead of one. In the palette, find Objective . Type objective in Filter nodes if the list is long. Drag it onto an empty part of the canvas. A new node appears. Click it. The inspector now shows its fields. Objective, in the palette. Fill in the two fields that matter for this exercise: Identifier A short name the game uses internally, like find-the-contact . The player never sees it, but it has to be unique within the quest. Shown to the player The line that appears in their quest panel. Write it as an instruction: Look up who wrote the article. Two fields, and the objective exists. Now wire it in. D"},{"url":"tutorial.html#wire-trigger","title":"Make it complete","section":"tutorial","text":"An objective with nothing wired to its trigger never completes on its own. This is the step that turns a quest from a script into something that reacts to the player. Drag When event from the palette onto the canvas. Click it and open its Game event field. Pick the event that matches what the player will do. Drag from the node's When socket to the objective's Trigger socket. The wire is cyan, because it is a different kind of connection from the blue ones. Mid-drag. The wire follows your cursor until you drop it on a matching socket. Choosing the event. There are 99, grouped by the part of the game they come from. The socket has to match. A cyan wire only lands on a cyan socket. If you drop "},{"url":"tutorial.html#write-the-reward","title":"Give the player a reason to finish","section":"tutorial","text":"Two nodes make a quest feel like a job rather than a checklist. Both are already in First Contact; click them to see what they hold. A Dialogue node is a conversation on the player's phone. Select one and the inspector shows who speaks and what they say. This is where an employer explains the job and where the story closes. A conversation, node by node. A Pay the player node puts money in their account. Set the amount in dollars. A hundred is a reasonable fee for a first job; the tools the player buys run around thirty-five, so the number should mean something to them. The payment. Leave the amount blank and the node does nothing."},{"url":"tutorial.html#check-it","title":"Check it before you ship it","section":"tutorial","text":"The editor checks your work in two places. The status bar along the bottom tells you the shape of what you have built. The status bar. It reports counts and whether your work is saved; it does not list problems. Problems show up on the nodes themselves and in the export window, not here. The fuller check is Dry run , which walks through your quest the way the exported mod would run it. Dry run. It is the closest thing to playing the quest without installing it. Run it and watch the order. If a node you expected never appears, it is not wired to anything. Drop a Debug probe after it and run again — the probe prints what a node does when it fires, and whether it fires at all. Checking your que"},{"url":"tutorial.html#export","title":"Export, install, play","section":"tutorial","text":"Click Export mod . The window that opens shows what you are about to ship before it packs anything: your quests, your websites, the permissions your nodes need, and every file that will go in the archive. The export summary. Read it before you download. Anything red means the game will drop or change something you built. Fix it first. Then choose Download .zip and unzip the folder into the game's mods/ directory. Installed. The quest is available to accept. Everything the archive contains and why is on the exporting and installing page. The one thing worth remembering from it: the .zip is finished output. Your project file is the thing you keep editing."},{"url":"tutorial.html#recap","title":"What you now know","section":"tutorial","text":"You loaded a quest, read what it did, added to it, made one objective respond to real play, and installed the result. Four ideas carried all of it: Wires decide order, not position. Moving a node changes nothing. Your quest map explains why. Wires have kinds. Blue for \"and then\", cyan for \"when\". A socket takes only its own kind. An objective completes when its trigger fires. That is the difference between a quest that reacts and one that assumes. Exporting is not saving. Two different files, two different purposes. Next, take apart a bigger template. The Byline adds a website clue, and Cold Call tells its story entirely in conversation. Then look at the How do I… pages when you want to buil"}];
